win32u: Honor the timeout when the queue stays signaled for driver events.

wait_message loops while the queue handle is signaled and
process_driver_events reports nothing to return for. The server keeps
the queue signaled while internal bits such as QS_DRIVER are set, but
the driver cannot process its events when it is called from inside its
own event processing, so the bits stay set and the loop spins without
end, whatever the timeout.

That happens with WPF: drag and drop calls reach the drop target from
inside the X11 driver's event processing, and WPF asks whether input
is pending with MsgWaitForMultipleObjectsEx(0, NULL, 0, QS_INPUT,
MWMO_INPUTAVAILABLE). With more X events queued, Affinity's UI thread
spun at full CPU inside DragOver or Drop until another thread happened
to wake the queue, for example on a focus change, and a file dropped on
its toolbar opened only then.

Return WAIT_TIMEOUT once the timeout has passed.
---
 dlls/win32u/message.c | 20 ++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dlls/win32u/message.c b/dlls/win32u/message.c
index 8c9a2aa..1a2882c 100644
--- a/dlls/win32u/message.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/message.c
@@ -3429,8 +3429,24 @@ static DWORD wait_message( DWORD count, const HANDLE *handles, DWORD timeout, DW
     process_driver_events( QS_ALLINPUT, wake_mask, changed_mask );
     if (!(changed_mask & QS_SMRESULT) && (event = get_user_thread_info()->idle_event)) NtSetEvent( event, NULL );
 
-    do ret = NtWaitForMultipleObjects( count, handles, type, !!(flags & MWMO_ALERTABLE), abs );
-    while (ret == count - 1 && !process_driver_events( QS_ALLINPUT, wake_mask, changed_mask ));
+    for (;;)
+    {
+        ret = NtWaitForMultipleObjects( count, handles, type, !!(flags & MWMO_ALERTABLE), abs );
+        if (ret != count - 1 || process_driver_events( QS_ALLINPUT, wake_mask, changed_mask )) break;
+        /* The queue stays signaled for driver events that cannot be processed here when we are
+         * called from inside the driver's own event processing, such as a drop target's DragOver
+         * calling MsgWaitForMultipleObjectsEx, which WPF does. Honor the timeout instead of
+         * spinning until another thread happens to wake the queue. */
+        if (abs)
+        {
+            NtQuerySystemTime( &now );
+            if (now.QuadPart >= abs->QuadPart)
+            {
+                ret = WAIT_TIMEOUT;
+                break;
+            }
+        }
+    }
 
     if (HIWORD(ret)) /* is it an error code? */
     {
